WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
Smart pointers are one of the most important tools in modern C++ memory and resource safety.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:17.000
They reduce the need for manual new and delete , make ownership visible in type signatures, and support RAII-based cleanup when code leaves a scope through normal execution, early return, or exception.

3
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.460
For security-minded engineering, that visibility matters.

4
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.160
Reviewers can understand who owns an object, who may borrow it, and where cleanup should happen.

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.600
Smart pointers are not magic.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:38.300
They do not prove that every lifetime is correct, that every resource is released at the intended time, or that every object is safe to access concurrently.

7
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:47.040
A system can still leak memory, retain sensitive objects longer than needed, create stale observations, or build ownership cycles that prevent cleanup.

8
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:55.140
Replacing raw pointers mechanically with smart pointers can even hide unclear ownership if the design questions are never answered.

9
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.220
The defensive goal is disciplined ownership design.

10
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.120
Choose value ownership when an object can live directly as a member or local variable.

11
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.460
Use exclusive ownership when one component controls lifetime.

12
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.520
Use shared ownership only when multiple owners genuinely need to keep an object alive.

13
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.620
Use weak observation when access should not extend lifetime.

14
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.120
Smart pointer security is the habit of making those decisions explicit, testable, and reviewable.

15
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.960
Throughout this course, treat smart pointers as one layer in secure C++ engineering.

16
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:42.860
They work best with clear API contracts, simple resource ownership, consistent construction patterns, tests around cleanup paths, diagnostics, and code review that follows the ownership graph.
